<v Clerk>海特坎普女士，反对。

00:53:16.940 --> 00:53:30.230
<v Presiding Officer>Is there any senator in the debate-- in the chamber that wishes to change their vote or vote? On this vote, the yeas are 51. The nays are 48. The concurrent resolution is agreed to.
<v Presiding Officer>辩论中——会议厅里有参议员希望改变他们的投票或进行投票吗？在这次投票中，赞成票51，反对票48。并发决议获得通过。

00:53:30.230 --> 00:53:34.340
<v Ira Glass>Act Six, Office Climate and Climate Office.
<v Ira Glass>第六幕，办公室气候与气候办公室（Office Climate and Climate Office）。

00:53:34.340 --> 00:53:56.390
<v Ira Glass>So any new president gets to appoint over 4,000 people to various positions, but most of the people in those big buildings in Washington DC are not those 4,000 people. They are civil servants. Most of them stay in their jobs from one administration to the next, whoever's in power. Not all of them. Our producer David Kestenbaum spoke with a couple of civil servants in DC who were deciding whether to stick around.
<v Ira Glass>所以任何新总统都能任命4000多人担任各种职位，但华盛顿特区那些大楼里的大多数人并非那4000人。他们是公务员。他们中的大多数人从一届政府到下一届政府都留在岗位上，无论谁当权。并非全部如此。我们的制作人戴维·凯斯滕鲍姆（David Kestenbaum）与华盛顿特区的几位公务员谈话，他们正在决定是否留下来。

00:53:56.390 --> 00:54:08.270
<v David Kestenbaum>Laura works at the Department of Energy, one of the agencies that seems like it might change dramatically under the new president. She asked us not to use her real name and to have an actor do her voice, even though she knows that's probably overkill.
<v David Kestenbaum>劳拉（Laura）在能源部工作，这是看起来在新总统领导下可能会发生巨大变化的机构之一。她要求我们不要使用她的真名，并让演员替她配音，尽管她知道那可能有些过度了。

00:54:08.270 --> 00:54:21.890
<v David Kestenbaum>She says as a civil servant you're supposed to keep your politics and your job separate. Your job is to enact the policies of whoever's in power, Democrat or Republican. But the first day back after the election it was hard to keep her politics to herself.
<v David Kestenbaum>她说作为一名公务员，你应该把政治和工作分开。你的工作是实施当权者的政策，无论是民主党还是共和党。但在选举后的第一天，她很难把政治倾向留给自己。

00:54:21.890 --> 00:54:36.740
<v Laura>I've never cried at work before. When you cry, you can't hide the fact afterwards-- at least for me, my face gets really red. So I had a meeting or whatever. I just had to be like, sorry, I just had a moment. And then everybody else was like, yeah, we did, too. It's not just you.
<v Laura>我以前从没在工作中哭过。当你哭的时候，你事后掩盖不了——至少对我来说，我的脸会变得很红。所以我开了一个会什么的。我只能说，抱歉，我刚才情绪波动了一下。然后其他人说，是啊，我们也一样。不只是你。

00:54:36.740 --> 00:54:40.340
<v David Kestenbaum>I don't want to get into too much detail, but your job is climate change-related?
<v David Kestenbaum>我不想讲太多细节，但你的工作是与气候变化相关的？

00:54:40.340 --> 00:54:41.270
<v Laura>Yes.
<v Laura>是的。

00:54:41.270 --> 00:54:48.260
<v David Kestenbaum>How many people at DoE do you think are unconvinced that humans are causing global warming?
<v David Kestenbaum>你认为能源部有多少人是不相信人类正在导致全球变暖的？

00:54:48.260 --> 00:54:51.510
<v Laura>I don't know anyone at DoE who thinks that.
<v Laura>我不认识能源部里认为那样的任何人。

00:54:51.510 --> 00:54:54.300
<v David Kestenbaum>Do you think it's literally zero?
<v David Kestenbaum>你认为真的是零吗？

00:54:54.300 --> 00:54:57.540
<v Laura>Probably zero or close to zero.
<v Laura>大概是零或者接近零。

00:54:57.540 --> 00:55:09.930
<v David Kestenbaum>It looked like that number would rise by at least one. Rick Perry, Trump's pick to run the DoE, had written in his own book that he thought the science showing humans were contributing to climate change was a, quote, "contrived phony mess."
<v David Kestenbaum>看起来那个数字至少会上升一个。特朗普挑选管理能源部的里克·佩里（Rick Perry），曾在自己的书中写道，他认为显示人类正在导致气候变化的科学是引用他的话，“虚构的假把戏”。

00:55:09.930 --> 00:55:20.340
<v David Kestenbaum>As you may have heard, Trump's team sent a list of questions to the DoE asking, among other things, the names of people who had attended UN climate change meetings. Democrats saw it as a kind of witch hunt.
<v David Kestenbaum>正如你可能听说的，特朗普的团队向能源部发送了一份问题清单，询问的内容包括参加联合国气候变化会议的人员名单。民主党人将其视为一种猎巫行动。

00:55:20.340 --> 00:55:36.380
<v David Kestenbaum>Rick Perry now says his views on climate change have changed and that he didn't approve of the questionnaire. But after the election, Laura spent a lot of time wondering, should I stay in this job? What would it be like? What if the new bosses ask me to do something I just think is wrong?
<v David Kestenbaum>里克·佩里现在说他对气候变化的看法已经改变，而且他没有批准那份调查问卷。但在选举后，劳拉花了很多时间思考，我应该留在这份工作中吗？会是什么样子？如果新老板让我做我仅仅认为是不对的事情怎么办？

00:55:36.380 --> 00:55:43.330
<v David Kestenbaum>Like what if there were some report or something and someone asked you to take out all the references to climate change?
<v David Kestenbaum>比如如果有某种报告，而有人要求你删除所有关于气候变化的引用？

00:55:43.330 --> 00:55:45.200
<v Laura>Well, we already did that.
<v Laura>嗯，我们已经那样做了。

00:55:45.200 --> 00:55:47.430
<v David Kestenbaum>You already did that?
<v David Kestenbaum>你们已经那样做了？

00:55:47.430 --> 00:55:49.440
<v Laura>Yeah, we have.
<v Laura>是的，我们已经做了。

00:55:49.440 --> 00:56:12.030
<v David Kestenbaum>Laura says she and a bunch of other people have been going through all their internal documents that describe ongoing projects and just scrubbing them, deleting the parts where it says, "and here's how this will help us combat climate change." Laura didn't want to talk specifics, but you could imagine a satellite for monitoring climate change and saying, think of it as a weather satellite instead. This renewable energy program? Now it's a jobs program.
<v David Kestenbaum>劳拉说她和一群其他人一直在梳理他们所有描述正在进行项目的内部文件，并进行清洗，删除那些写着“这里是我们如何帮助应对气候变化”的部分。劳拉不想谈具体内容，但你可以想象一颗用于监测气候变化的卫星，并说，把它当作一颗气象卫星。这个可再生能源项目？现在它是一个就业项目。

00:56:12.030 --> 00:56:19.280
<v David Kestenbaum>Most federal projects have a bunch of reasons for their existence. Why draw attention to something by putting the words "climate change" in the description?
<v David Kestenbaum>大多数联邦项目存在都有很多原因。为什么要通过在描述中放上“气候变化”这些词来引人注意呢？

00:56:19.280 --> 00:56:21.660
<v David Kestenbaum>Did it feel sad to take it out?
<v David Kestenbaum>删除它感觉难过吗？

00:56:21.660 --> 00:56:22.740
<v Laura>No.
<v Laura>不。

00:56:22.740 --> 00:56:25.300
<v David Kestenbaum>Because you thought, this is a way we can get by?
<v David Kestenbaum>因为你心想，这是我们可以过关的一种方式？

00:56:25.300 --> 00:56:35.270
<v Laura>Yeah, exactly. That was my thought. As long as it's getting done, it doesn't matter what we call it.
<v Laura>是的，正是如此。那是我的想法。只要事情完成了，叫什么名字并不重要。

00:56:35.270 --> 00:56:53.370
<v David Kestenbaum>Laura's entire time at the DoE has been under Obama. She's never been through a change of power before. That first day after the election, there was this big all-staff meeting at the DoE. That made her feel a little better-- not because it was emotional, but because it wasn't. It was professional, just like, here's what happens next.
<v David Kestenbaum>劳拉在能源部的整个时间都是在奥巴马领导下。她以前从没经历过权力更迭。选举后的第一天，能源部举行了一次全体员工大会。那让她感觉好一点——不是因为它很情绪化，而是因为它很专业，就像，这是接下来会发生的事情。

00:56:53.370 --> 00:57:04.770
<v Laura>I think what made it OK was it was very matter-of-fact. Like, the transition team is going to come in in the next few days. They didn't. They came in like several weeks later.
<v Laura>我认为让它没关系的原因是它非常公事公办。就像，过渡团队将在接下来的几天内进来。他们没有。他们是在几周后进来的。

00:57:04.770 --> 00:57:19.860
<v David Kestenbaum>When the transition team did arrive at DoE, Laura went on what felt like a kid detective mission to try to see the invaders. She knew they were on the fifth floor, so she went up there with a friend. They tried to play it cool, had to do a couple laps because they missed the room the first time. But there it was.
<v David Kestenbaum>当过渡团队到达能源部时，劳拉进行了一次感觉像孩子般的侦探任务，试图看看这些入侵者。她知道他们在五楼，所以她和朋友一起上去了。她们试图保持冷静，因为第一次错过了房间，不得不绕了两圈。但它就在那里。

00:57:19.860 --> 00:57:31.970
<v Laura>It literally said on the door, "transition team." And I elbowed my friend, and she was like, I saw it. It wasn't like something was hidden or something, but I felt very like, oh, wow. There it is.
<v Laura>门上真的写着“过渡团队”。我用胳膊肘碰了碰我的朋友，她说，我看到了。它不像有什么东西被隐藏起来，但我感觉非常，哦，哇。它就在那里。

00:57:31.970 --> 00:57:35.400
<v David Kestenbaum>Somehow seeing the office make you feel more reassured?
<v David Kestenbaum>某种程度上看到办公室让你感到更放心？

00:57:35.400 --> 00:57:46.230
<v Laura>Yeah, because the name was on the door. It's one of these things. It's like, well, they're not trying to hide anything. They're just there doing their job.
<v Laura>是的，因为名字写在门上。这是这些事情之一。就像，好吧，他们没试图隐瞒任何东西。他们只是在那里做他们的工作。

00:57:46.230 --> 00:58:10.810
<v David Kestenbaum>I talked to this other government worker who we'll call Karen. Karen's a relatively senior person. She served under a Democrat and a Republican. Karen says a bunch of younger coworkers have been coming into her office, closing the door, and saying, do you have a minute to talk? It's like she's become a therapist. Some of the people are trying to figure out what to do-- stay or leave. If you stay, at least you can try to steer things. Or you can fight.
<v David Kestenbaum>我和另一位我们将称为凯伦（Karen）的政府工作人员谈过。凯伦是一位相对资深的人士。她在民主党人和共和党人手下任职。凯伦说一群年轻的同事一直在走进她的办公室，关上门，说，你有空谈谈吗？就像她变成了一名治疗师。有些人正在试图弄清楚该做什么——留下还是离开。如果你留下，至少你可以尝试引导事情。或者你可以战斗。

00:58:10.810 --> 00:58:22.700
<v David Kestenbaum>And by fight, I mean that in the most bureaucratic way possible. Government moves very slowly, which is a pain when you're trying to get things done. But if you're trying to stop something from happening, it can be used to your advantage.
<v David Kestenbaum>通过战斗，我是指用尽可能官僚的方式。政府行动非常缓慢，当你试图把事情做完时这很痛苦。但如果你试图阻止事情发生，它可以成为你的优势。

00:58:22.700 --> 00:58:49.960
<v Karen>Withholding information is one way you slow things down. The bureaucracy is large. There's a lot of paperwork, a lot of steps, and people that have been in government for a long time understand all those steps really well. So some of those tactics may be used to make things go a lot slower.
<v Karen>隐瞒信息是你放慢速度的一种方式。官僚机构很庞大。有很多文书工作，很多步骤，而在政府工作了很长时间的人非常了解所有这些步骤。所以其中一些战术可能会被用来让事情进行得慢得多。

00:58:49.960 --> 00:59:01.860
<v David Kestenbaum>One government worker told me he knew some people who were really good at this. It's like the dark arts of civil service. You can refer things to the general counsel for legal review. That takes time. You can also try to hide things.
<v David Kestenbaum>一名政府工作人员告诉我，他认识一些非常擅长此道的人。这就像公务员制的暗黑艺术。你可以把事情提交给法律顾问进行法律审查。那需要时间。你也可以尝试隐瞒事情。

00:59:01.860 --> 00:59:12.320
<v David Kestenbaum>One relatively senior official who recently left government has been advising those who stayed behind to just lay low, keep any controversial stuff under the radar. Karen knows that trick.
<v David Kestenbaum>一位最近刚离开政府的相对资深官员一直建议那些留下的人保持低调，把任何有争议的东西都置于雷达之下。凯伦知道那个诀窍。

00:59:12.320 --> 00:59:19.810
<v Karen>It's not like you can come into the government and, with the click of a button, find everything you need to know on any topic we've worked on for the last 15 years.
<v Karen>不像你可以走进政府，按一下按钮，就能找到你在过去15年里所做工作的任何主题所需的一切信息。

00:59:19.810 --> 00:59:28.390
<v David Kestenbaum>So literally, there might be like programs or documents or things that are just so well hidden that they'd never find out about them?
<v David Kestenbaum>所以字面上讲，可能有一些计划或文件或事情只是被藏得太好，以至于他们永远都不会发现它们？

00:59:28.390 --> 00:59:30.460
<v Karen>You could say that.
<v Karen>你可以这样说。

00:59:30.460 --> 00:59:34.540
<v David Kestenbaum>Are people talking about doing that kind of stuff?
<v David Kestenbaum>人们在讨论做那种事情吗？

00:59:34.540 --> 00:59:49.690
<v Karen>I think people don't know yet how they will react because they are waiting to see if what they are predicting may happen does come to pass.
<v Karen>我认为人们还不知道他们将如何反应，因为他们正在等待看看他们所预测可能会发生的事情是否真的会发生。

00:59:49.690 --> 00:59:51.400
<v David Kestenbaum>But if it does?
<v David Kestenbaum>但如果它真的发生了呢？

00:59:51.400 --> 01:00:14.750
<v Karen>There are definitely some career civil servants that will not ever give in, and I think there are definitely some career civil servants that will toe the line. But I think the people that know how the system works have used these tactics within many administrations.
<v Karen>肯定有一些职业公务员永远不会屈服，而且我认为肯定有一些职业公务员会循规蹈矩。但我认为了解系统如何运作的人已经在许多届政府中使用了这些战术。

01:00:14.750 --> 01:00:21.500
<v David Kestenbaum>Sounds kind of wrong. It doesn't seem like the right thing to do.
<v David Kestenbaum>听起来有点不对。那看起来不像是一件正确的事情。

01:00:21.500 --> 01:00:49.180
<v Karen>I think when you've worked on something for a long time and you've devoted your entire career to it and you believe it is the right thing to do, people will do their jobs. But when they think that what they are doing is harmful to citizens or the country in the long run, I think they will stand up for what they believe in the bureaucratic ways that they can.
<v Karen>我认为当你长期从事某件事，并将你的整个职业生涯都致力于它，并且你相信它是正确的事情时，人们会做好他们的工作。但当他们认为他们正在做的事情在长远来看对公民或国家有害时，我认为他们会用他们能做到的官僚方式为他们所相信的事情挺身而出。

01:00:49.180 --> 01:01:04.330
<v David Kestenbaum>Karen is not going to be doing any of that. I imagine that's true of most people. They'll either do their jobs like good civil servants or they'll just leave. Karen is leaving. It didn't take her long to decide. She knew almost immediately, on election night.
<v David Kestenbaum>凯伦不会做任何那种事情。我想对大多数人来说也是如此。他们要么会像优秀的公务员一样做好工作，要么就会离开。凯伦要离开了。她没花多长时间就决定了。她几乎立刻就知道了，在选举之夜。

01:01:04.330 --> 01:01:25.390
<v Karen>I remember saying to a close friend, wow, I'll be departing from my job in January. And I'm a career public servant. [SOBS] I'm sorry.
<v Karen>我记得对一位亲密的朋友说，哇，我一月份就要离开我的工作了。我是职业公务员。（啜泣）抱歉。

01:01:25.390 --> 01:01:44.120
<v David Kestenbaum>Laura, at the Department of Energy, took longer to make up her mind. Senior people there-- Democrats-- were urging people to stay under Trump. Don't leave, they said. We need good people here. If you leave, you don't know who will replace you. And you don't know how bad it'll be. Maybe it'll be OK.
<v David Kestenbaum>能源部的劳拉花了更长时间才拿定主意。那里的资深人士——民主党人——正在敦促人们在特朗普领导下留下。别走，他们说。我们需要这里有好的人。如果你走了，你不知道谁会取代你。而且你不知道会变得多糟。也许会没事的。

01:01:44.120 --> 01:01:56.610
<v David Kestenbaum>It was that questionnaire that sealed things for her, the one that made the news and asked for the names of people who had attended UN climate change talks. Laura wasn't in the office when the news broke. She heard about it by email from a friend in another part of government.
<v David Kestenbaum>是那份调查问卷让她最终决定，那份上了新闻并要求提供参加联合国气候变化谈话人员名单的问卷。消息传出时劳拉不在办公室。她是通过政府另一部分的朋友发的电子邮件听说的。

01:01:56.610 --> 01:02:04.380
<v Laura>Well, she was like, this doesn't look good, basically. And I was like, on the contrary, this is kind of funny.
<v Laura>嗯，她说，这看起来不妙，基本上。而我回答说，恰恰相反，这还有点滑稽。

01:02:04.380 --> 01:02:09.210
<v David Kestenbaum>It seemed like such an amateur move. The DoE, by the way, refused to provide any names.
<v David Kestenbaum>这看起来像是非常业余的举动。顺便说一句，能源部拒绝提供任何名单。

01:02:09.210 --> 01:02:19.720
<v Laura>That just seemed like a scare tactic to me, and I didn't want to reward that sort of brazen attempt to get people to leave the agency. I'm not leaving.
<v Laura>那对我来说只是恐吓手段，我不想奖励那种想让人们离开该机构的厚颜无耻的企图。我不会走的。

01:02:19.720 --> 01:02:22.320
<v David Kestenbaum>Are you thinking like, I can last four years?
<v David Kestenbaum>你在想就像，我能坚持四年？

01:02:22.320 --> 01:02:26.020
<v Laura>I think I can last four years.
<v Laura>我想我能坚持四年。

01:02:26.020 --> 01:02:28.240
<v David Kestenbaum>Could you last eight?
<v David Kestenbaum>你能坚持八年吗？

01:02:28.240 --> 01:02:31.440
<v Laura>I probably wouldn't stay for eight, no.
<v Laura>我可能不会留八年，不。

01:02:31.440 --> 01:02:33.780
<v David Kestenbaum>What are you doing for the inauguration?
<v David Kestenbaum>你就职典礼那天打算做什么？

01:02:33.780 --> 01:02:38.070
<v Laura>Probably hanging out with my friends in a bar.
<v Laura>大概和朋友们在酒吧里闲逛。

01:02:38.070 --> 01:02:40.020
<v David Kestenbaum>Watching it or avoiding it?
<v David Kestenbaum>看它还是避免它？

01:02:40.020 --> 01:02:46.690
<v Laura>Not watching it. I've already seen enough of him to last a lifetime.
<v Laura>不看。我已经见过他足够多了，足以维持一辈子了。

01:02:46.690 --> 01:02:50.230
<v David Kestenbaum>Well, she's going to be seeing more of him, as she knows.
<v David Kestenbaum>嗯，正如她所知，她还会见到更多他。

01:02:50.230 --> 01:03:01.240
<v Laura>We have these pictures hanging up in every office of the president, the vice president, the secretary of energy, and they're all going to change.
<v Laura>我们每间办公室里都挂着总统、副总统、能源部长的照片，而且它们都要换了。

01:03:01.240 --> 01:03:04.090
<v David Kestenbaum>You're going to have a picture of Donald Trump in your office?
<v David Kestenbaum>你办公室里会挂一张唐纳德·特朗普的照片吗？

01:03:04.090 --> 01:03:12.740
<v Laura>Yes. And Mike Pence and Rick Perry and whoever the undersecretary is.
<v Laura>是的。还有迈克·彭斯（Mike Pence）和里克·佩里，以及无论谁是副部长。

01:03:12.740 --> 01:03:14.230
<v David Kestenbaum>How do you feel about that?
<v David Kestenbaum>你对此感觉如何？

01:03:14.230 --> 01:03:35.630
<v Laura>I don't know. At least-- I mean, like-- that's like-- [SIGHS] it's just a reminder of the world we live in every day. I think you can bury your head a little bit and try to keep doing what you're doing, but there are people you answer to and that picture makes you remember who they are.
<v Laura>我不知道。至少——我的意思是，就像——那是就像——（叹气）这只是我们每天生活的世界的提醒。我想你可以把头稍微埋一下，尝试继续做你正在做的事，但有些人是你需要负责的，而那张照片让你记住他们是谁。

01:03:35.630 --> 01:04:06.050
<v David Kestenbaum>We looked into it. The portraits of Obama and his officials were scheduled to be removed on Friday at noon, the exact time Donald Trump took the oath of office. We were told the pictures would be disposed of respectfully. In my mind, there's a big dumpster somewhere with all of the photos in it, like an actual dustbin of history. The frames do get saved and reused for the new portraits. They're going to be going up in the next few weeks.
<v David Kestenbaum>我们查了一下。奥巴马及其官员的肖像原定于周五中午，即唐纳德·特朗普宣誓就职的准确时间被移除。我们被告知这些照片会被尊重地处理。在我看来，某个地方有一个巨大的垃圾箱，里面装着所有的照片，就像一个真正的历史尘埃桶。框子会被保存并重新利用用于新的肖像。它们将在接下来的几周内挂上去。

01:04:06.050 --> 01:04:45.920
<v Ira Glass>David Kestenbaum is one of the producers of our program. The actor who performed Laura's quotes is Jen Davis.
<v Ira Glass>戴维·凯斯滕鲍姆是我们节目的制作人之一。扮演劳拉引言的演员是简·戴维斯（Jen Davis）。

01:04:45.920 --> 01:05:04.210
<v Ira Glass>Our program was produced today by David Kestenbaum. Our production staff includes Susan Burton, Zoe Chace, Dana Chivvas, Sean Cole, Neil Drumming, Karen Duffin, Emmanuel Dzotsi, Stephanie Foo, Chana Joffe-Walt, Miki Meek, Jonathan Menjivar, Robyn Semien, Matt Tierney, Nancy Updike, and Diane Wu. Research help today from Christopher Swetala and Michelle Harris. Music help today from Damian Graef.
<v Ira Glass>我们今天的节目由戴维·凯斯滕鲍姆制作。我们的制作人员包括苏珊·伯顿（Susan Burton）、佐伊·蔡斯、达纳·奇瓦斯（Dana Chivvas）、肖恩·科尔（Sean Cole）、尼尔·德拉明（Neil Drumming）、凯伦·达芬（Karen Duffin）、伊曼纽尔·佐齐（Emmanuel Dzotsi）、斯蒂芬妮·富、查娜·乔夫-沃尔特（Chana Joffe-Walt）、米基·米克（Miki Meek）、乔纳森·门吉瓦尔（Jonathan Menjivar）、罗宾·塞米恩（Robyn Semien）、马特·蒂尔尼（Matt Tierney）、南希·厄普代克（Nancy Updike）和戴安·吴（Diane Wu）。今天的研究帮助来自克里斯托弗·斯韦塔拉（Christopher Swetala）和米歇尔·哈里斯（Michelle Harris）。今天的音乐帮助来自达米安·格拉夫（Damian Graef）。

01:05:04.210 --> 01:05:21.100
<v Ira Glass>[ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS]
<v Ira Glass>（致谢）

01:05:21.100 --> 01:05:37.940
<v Ira Glass>Our website, thisamericanlife.org. This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange. Thanks as always to our show's co-founder Mr. Torey Malatia. He does not understand why the president's motorcade has to cause so much traffic whenever he's driving around New York heading to Trump Tower.
<v Ira Glass>我们的网站，thisamericanlife.org。《美国生活》由公共广播交易所（PRX）分发给公共广播电台。像往常一样感谢我们节目的联合创始人托里·马拉蒂亚（Torey Malatia）先生。他不明白为什么总统的车队无论何时在纽约开车前往特朗普大厦时都要造成那么严重的交通拥堵。

01:05:37.940 --> 01:05:39.720
<v Kenia>Like, he has legs.
<v Kenia>就像，他有腿。

01:05:39.720 --> 01:05:44.720
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周带着更多《美国生活》的故事回来。
